old":0.5,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Confirm the fail-open repeat-fix history read is acceptable, or emit a distinguishable signal (e.g. coverage note) when the git walk fails so 'checked and clean' is separable from 'could not check'.","criteria":["error-handling"],"decision":"dropped","evidence":["src/rebar/llm/code_review/repeat_fix.py:52 `if proc.returncode != 0:` then `return None`","`if not out: return []` in _commits_touching (repeat_fix.py:77)","module docstring: 'The predicate is deliberately *fail-open*: any git or store trouble yields \"no escalation\" rather than an exception'","Litmus: the (False, []) verdict is produced both when the path is genuinely clean AND when git failed / the walk never ran — the two are not separable by any caller"],"finding":"The repeat-fix predicate cannot distinguish 'git could not read history' from 'the path has no prior fixes': _git() returns None on any git/OS error or non-zero returncode, _commits_touching() returns [] for that None, and repeat_fix_escalates() then returns (False, []). A genuine inability to read the base branch history therefore reports the same 'no escalation' verdict as a clean history. This is documented as an intentional fail-open, but the check's failure is invisible and its clean verdict is indistinguishable from the check never having run.","id":"0","impact":0.0,"location":"src/rebar/llm/code_review/repeat_fix.py:40","norm_id":"n61ec85c78644728c","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","scenarios":["A misconfigured or partial checkout where `git log <base_ref>` fails silently — every repeat fix passes as if it had no history"],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.4286,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"y